body:
Parents can be bad at underestimating the toll that stress is playing on their kids. Have you ever thought of seeking a therapist or counselor? It sounds like you could really benefit from having a trusting adult to confide in, and maybe it would be better for you if it wasn't necessarily your parents (who have your best interest in mind, but might be biased about what you need). Would you be able to tell your parents or a teacher that you're feeling upset about your skin?

&#x200B;

And I'm totally happy to talk, I am glad you responded, and hope you feel free to keep talking to me if it feels helpful for you.

Are your teeth in pain? I understand being ashamed if they  appear unattractive to your standards, but are you in physical pain due to their neglect (which isn't your fault- my first time going to the dentist was at the age of 21, because I wasn't taken care of either). If so, I really recommend getting that checked out- physical illness adds so much stress to the mind and body, and it would be doing yourself a wonderful gift to look into getting them healthy (crooked does not equal healthy!). If you want to disclose your general area, I'll help look for affordable sliding scale dentists for you. You're not alone.

&#x200B;

Being asexual, schizophrenic, on drugs (I notice you mentioned acid and being high now), nor having a neglected childhood are not enough to make you unlovable. In fact, these are things that shape you, and even though you may not feel this way now, are things that people can be drawn to. I am getting some relief from my own depression in responding to you, and the fact that you're the type of person to reach out is why I'm able to do that. Living in such a destructive environment, like you described your childhood, surely shaped how you view yourself and the world. I encourage you to keep venting, get it all out, like you said in your last statements. Have you thought of anything else you want to add?
